[Line "Milner-Barry Gambit: 6.Bd3 cxd4 7.cxd4 Bd7 8.O-O, main line 11.Re1 Qd6 12.Nb5"]
[Section "5...Qb6 6.Bd3: Milner-Barry Gambit"]
[Summary "White gives up d4 and e5 for a lead in development. The pin on the e6-pawn and the knight jumps to b5 and d5 keep Black's queen and king in trouble. The first thing to know is that 7...Nxd4 loses the queen."]

1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. e5 c5 4. c3 Nc6 5. Nf3 Qb6 6. Bd3 { [%cal Gd3b5,Rb6d4] **The idea:** White does not add a
defender to d4, and the bishop develops with a threat of its own: if Black takes twice on d4, with the knight and then
the queen, Bb5+ is a discovered attack that wins the queen. The gambit is named after Stuart Milner-Barry; it is a main line in
Marin's course and the subject of Robert Ris's ChessBase video on its improved form. It is the sharp second weapon
of this chapter: whoever wants no quiet lines at all can play 6.Bd3 against 5...Qb6 every time. }
6... cxd4 { Black takes the pawn. }
7. cxd4 { [%csl Rd4] The pawn recaptures. A knight recapture would leave e5 without a defender. }
( 7. Nxd4? { Takes with the knight. } 7... Nxe5 { Black wins a pawn and has a healthy position. } )
7... Bd7! { [%cal Gd7e8] The right move. It blocks the check on b5, and now the threat of ...Nxd4 is real. }
( 7... Nxd4? { The tempting capture, and the trap that makes the whole line worth playing. } 8. Nxd4 Qxd4?? { Takes the knight and walks into it. } 9. Bb5+!
{ [%cal Gb5e8,Gd1d4] The check is a discovered attack: the bishop leaves d3 and the white queen suddenly looks at the
black one. } 9... Bd7 10. Bxd7+ Kxd7 11. Qxd4 { White has won the queen. Wikipedia and Wikibooks, Levy's course and
the Lichess studies on the gambit all show it. } )
8. O-O { [%cal Gf1e1,Gb1c3] **The gambit:** White castles and lets Black take the knight on d4. In return he gets an
open e-file, the knight route to d5 and b5, and a lead in development. }
( 8. Be2 { Declines the gambit and gets a much less ambitious position (Wikibooks). } )
8... Nxd4 { Black takes the pawn with the knight; this is the normal move. } 9. Nxd4 { The knight trade is the
main line. Marin also examines 9.Nbd2 and 9.Ng5, which have their own game below. } 9... Qxd4 { The queen recaptures. }
10. Nc3 { [%cal Gc3b5,Gc3d5,Gf1e1] The knight eyes d5 and b5, and Re1 is threatened. White has given a second pawn:
10...Qxe5 is possible. }
10... Qxe5 { The natural move and the one Black plays most often. }
11. Re1 { [%cal Ge1e5,Ge1e6] The queen is attacked, and once she leaves the e-file the e6-pawn is pinned to the king,
so Black can never recapture on d5 with it. All the tactics below are built on that pin. }
( 11. Bf4?! { Not now: the queen takes the bishop. } 11... Qxf4 )
11... Qd6 { A popular reply (Wikipedia, the Lichess study): the queen guards d5 and steps out of the pin. }
12. Nb5 { [%cal Gb5c7,Gb5d6] The knight attacks the queen and c7 at once. Black must decide where the queen goes
before Be3 or Bf4 and Rc1 arrive. }
12... Qb6 { The queen keeps b2 in sight. }
( 12... Qb8 { The queen goes to the corner. } 13. Qf3 { [%cal Gc1f4] **Idea:** the queen guards the future bishop
square f4, so Bf4 can come with tempo. Bf4 at once would just lose the bishop to the queen. } 13... Bd6 { Black
prevents the fork. The source says that after trading on d6 and bringing out the bishop White still has a good,
active position. } )
13. Be3! { [%cal Ge3b6,Gb5d6] The bishop attacks the queen with tempo and develops. }
( 13. Bf4? { Looks natural, because the fork on c7 is threatened. } 13... Bxb5 14. Bxb5+ Qxb5 { The knight on b5 has
two attackers, so the idea fails and White loses a piece. } )
13... Bc5? { [%cal Gc5e3] A very common blunder at this point (the Lichess study). } 14. Bxc5! { Take the bishop first
and play Rc1 afterwards: 14.Rc1? gives away too many ideas. } 14... Qxc5 15. Rc1 { [%cal Gc1c5,Gb5c7] The queen is pinned
against the rook and the knight threatens Nc7+. } 15... Qb6 16. Nc7+ Ke7 17. Nxd5+ Kf8 18. Nxb6 { **Verdict:** White
wins the queen. This is the line as club players meet it. Against exact defence (the next games) the gambit is
compensation, not a refutation of 5...Qb6, and Wikibooks rates the position as level. } *

[Line "Milner-Barry: Black's best defence 11...Qb8 and other queen moves"]
[Section "5...Qb6 6.Bd3: Milner-Barry Gambit"]
[Summary "Giving back the d5-pawn with 11...Qb8 is Black's best, but Qh5 keeps the initiative. The other queen moves lose to tactics on the pinned e6-pawn and the d7-bishop."]

1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. e5 c5 4. c3 Nc6 5. Nf3 Qb6 6. Bd3 cxd4 7. cxd4 Bd7 8. O-O Nxd4 9. Nxd4 Qxd4 10. Nc3 Qxe5
11. Re1 Qb8 { The queen retreats and protects c7. This is the correct move, and the Lichess study says it is
played only about a tenth of the time. } 12. Nxd5 { [%cal Gd5c7,Gd5f6] The e6-pawn is pinned, so the knight cannot be
taken; Nc7+ and Nf6+ are in the air. } 12... Bd6 { The best defence (the study). } 13. Qh5! { [%cal Gh5e8,Gh5f7] The
queen adds a second pin on the h5-e8 diagonal; the study calls it hard to find and the position very uncomfortable
for Black even with accurate play. **Plan:** keep the knight protected on d5 and bring Bf4 and Rc1. }
( 13. h3 { The study gives it as an alternative: it protects h2 and loses no time. Roughly as good, but less
tricky. } )
( 13. Qg4 { Still tricky, but h2 is loose. } )
( 13. g3 { Prevents the bishop's battery on h2, but does little else and may weaken the kingside; in the study's
statistics Black scores better than White after it. } )
{ **Verdict:** the study says that Black's position is so uncomfortable that White usually wins in practice.
The source gives no moves after 13.Qh5, so the line ends here. } *

[Line "Milner-Barry: 11...Qc7, 11...Qf6 and 11...Qd4"]
[Section "5...Qb6 6.Bd3: Milner-Barry Gambit"]
[Summary "The other ways the queen can leave the pin fail to tactics: the pinned e6-pawn, the loose d7-bishop and knight forks on c7."]

1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. e5 c5 4. c3 Nc6 5. Nf3 Qb6 6. Bd3 cxd4 7. cxd4 Bd7 8. O-O Nxd4 9. Nxd4 Qxd4 10. Nc3 Qxe5
11. Re1 Qc7 { The queen protects c7 and the d7-bishop. } 12. Nxd5 { [%cal Gd5c7] The pinned pawn cannot take. }
12... Qc6 { Black attacks the knight a second time. }
( 12... Qd6 { The queen guards d5 again. } 13. Bc4 { [%cal Gc4e6] The knight is protected twice. } 13... Be7 14. Bf4 Qc5
15. Nc7+ Kf8 16. Nxa8 Qxc4 17. Qxd7 )
13. Bb5! { [%cal Gb5c6,Gd5c7] Whichever piece takes loses something: ...Qxb5 allows Nc7+ with a fork of king and
queen, and ...Qxd5 is met by Qxd5. }
( 13. Be4 { Also good: the bishop breaks the pin on its own terms. } )
13... Qd6 14. Bf4 { [%cal Gf4d6] The queen is attacked again. } 14... Qc5 15. Nc7+ Kd8 16. Qxd7# { **Verdict:** a model
of the tactics in this structure: pins on the e-file and the d7-bishop, and a knight that forks from c7. } *

[Line "Milner-Barry: 11...Qf6 and 11...Qd4"]
[Section "5...Qb6 6.Bd3: Milner-Barry Gambit"]
[Summary "Two more queen moves that look natural and fail: the queen on f6 runs into the second pin on the h5-e8 diagonal, and the queen on d4 into a discovered attack."]

1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. e5 c5 4. c3 Nc6 5. Nf3 Qb6 6. Bd3 cxd4 7. cxd4 Bd7 8. O-O Nxd4 9. Nxd4 Qxd4 10. Nc3 Qxe5
11. Re1 Qf6 { The queen steps out of the pin and attacks the knight on c3. }
( 11... Qd4 { Rare, but the tactic is too pretty to skip. } 12. Rxe6+ { [%cal Gd3b5,Gd1d4] Whatever Black takes with,
there is a check with a discovered attack on the queen: ...Bxe6 is met by Bb5+ and ...fxe6 by Bg6+. } 12... Be7 { Black
blocks instead of taking; White continues the attack on the queen with Nb5 or Be3. } )
12. Nxd5 { [%cal Gd5f6] The knight takes with an attack on the queen. } 12... Qd8 13. Bf4 { [%cal Gf4c7] Nc7+ is the threat,
and the bishop supports it. } 13... Rc8 14. Rc1 { Brings the last piece to the attack. } 14... Bc6 15. Qh5 { [%cal Gh5e8,Ge1e6]
A second pin: the e6-pawn and the bishop are both tied, and Black is likely to err. } 15... Qxd5 16. Qxd5 Bxd5 17. Rxc8+
{ **Verdict:** the rook on c8 is no longer protected once the queens are off, so Black cannot take the knight on d5.
It takes a lot of mistakes to get here, but the sources show that this is how club games are lost. } *

[Line "Milner-Barry: 10...a6 11.g3"]
[Section "5...Qb6 6.Bd3: Milner-Barry Gambit"]
[Summary "Black declines the second pawn and stops Nb5 with ...a6. White reroutes with g3 and Bf4; Qe2 with Kh1 and f4 is the main alternative."]

1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. e5 c5 4. c3 Nc6 5. Nf3 Qb6 6. Bd3 cxd4 7. cxd4 Bd7 8. O-O Nxd4 9. Nxd4 Qxd4 10. Nc3 a6 { [%csl Rb5]
Black knows that taking the second pawn is tricky, and prevents Nb5 first. The natural 11.Re1 can run into
problems (the Lichess study). }
11. g3 { [%cal Gc1f4,Gg3f4] A rare move with shocking potential (the Lichess study 'French as White'): the pawn
protects f4, so the bishop can go there even when the queen takes on e5. Odds are about even in the source. }
( 11. Qe2 { Considered the main line by the study. The idea is Kh1 and f4, with the rook staying on f1 to support
the push; it works out if you know what you are doing. } )
( 11. Ne2 { Playable on paper, but it scores very badly in practice according to the study. } )
11... Qxe5 { Black takes the second pawn anyway. } 12. Re1 Qd6 { Nb5 is not available, but the pawn on g3 gives the
bishop a new route. } 13. Bf4 Qc6 14. Rc1 Be7 15. Nb5 { [%cal Gb5c7,Gb5d6,Gf4c7] } 15... Qb6 16. Bc7 { **Verdict:** the queen is
trapped on b6 and Black loses it for less than it is worth; 15...Qb6 does not stop the fork and has a second
problem in Bc7. } *

[Line "Milner-Barry Hector Gambit: 6...cxd4 7.O-O"]
[Section "5...Qb6 6.Bd3: Milner-Barry Gambit"]
[Summary "White does not recapture on d4 at all and castles first. The second pawn goes on c3, and the pieces come out faster still. A sharper form of the gambit."]

1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. e5 c5 4. c3 Nc6 5. Nf3 Qb6 6. Bd3 cxd4 7. O-O { [%cal Gf1e1,Gb1c3,Gc1e3] The Hector Gambit, the
'improved' Milner-Barry of Robert Ris's ChessBase video (White quickly pushes the h-pawn there). The Lichess
studies 'Crush the French Defense' (39 likes) and 'French as White' (25 likes) both show it. }
7... dxc3 { Black takes the second pawn. }
( 7... Bd7 { The most common reply (Chess in the Library and the study 'Crush the French Defense'): Black keeps the pawn
on d4 and covers b5. 8.cxd4 transposes to the main line, and 8.Re1 keeps the gambit going. } 8. Re1 { A waiting move that
keeps all the ideas. } 8... dxc3 9. Nxc3 Nge7 10. Be3 { If Black does not play ...d4, the same ideas as in the Hector main line
apply. } 10... d4? { The natural-looking pawn push, which loses material to a pin. } 11. Nxd4 Nxd4 12. Bxd4 Qxd4?
{ Takes the bishop and walks into the discovered attack. } 13. Nb5 { [%cal Gb5d4,Gb5c7] The knight attacks the queen, and
whatever Black does, the bishop on d3 delivers a discovered check. } 13... Bxb5 14. Bxb5+ ) 8. Nxc3 { [%cal Gc3b5,Gc3d5]
The knight comes in with a hit on d5. White has given two pawns and every piece aims at the black king. } 8... Bd7
{ [%cal Gd7b5] Black develops and covers b5. }
( 8... a6 9. Be3 Qc7 10. Na4 { [%cal Ga4b6,Gc3c5] } 10... Nge7 ( 10... Nxe5? { Grabs a third pawn. } 11. Nxe5 Qxe5 12. Nb6 Rb8 13. Qa4+ { The king has to move and White crushes (Chess in the Library). } )
11. Rc1 Ng6 12. Bb6! { Chases the queen to b8, and 13.Re1 then keeps the e5-pawn defended. } 12... Qb8 13. Re1 )
9. Be3 { [%cal Ge3b6,Gc3b5] The bishop attacks the queen and gives b2 away on purpose. }
9... Qd8 { The queen retreats at once and keeps the extra pawns. }
( 9... Qxb2 { The greedy move. } 10. Nb5 { [%cal Gb5c7,Ga1b1] The knight threatens Nc7+. } 10... Rc8 11. Re1
{ Preparing Re2, which attacks the queen. } 11... a6 12. Rb1 Qxa2 13. Re2 Qa5 14. Bb6!! { [%cal Gb6a5,Gb1b6] The queen is
trapped. } 14... Qxb6 15. Nd6+ Bxd6 16. Rxb6 )
( 9... d4? { Attacks two pieces. } 10. Nxd4 Nxd4 11. Qg4 { The bishop on e3 and the queen both hit the knight on
d4, and Qxg7 is behind it. } 11... Bc5?? { The most common move in the source, and it loses at once. } 12. Qxg7 )
( 9... Bc5?? { Natural development, but the bishop and the queen are both exposed to the knight. } 10. Na4 )
10. Bd2 { [%cal Gd2c3] Chess in the Library prefers this to 10.Rc1: it protects the knight, so ...Bb4 and ...Bxc3 are
not possible. } 10... Nge7 11. Bg5 { [%cal Gc3b5] } 11... a6! { Black's best (Chess in the Library): the pawn takes b5 from the knight in advance. } ( 11... h6?? { The
most common mistake. } 12. Nb5 { Threatens Nd6+ with mate ideas. }
12... Qb6 13. Nd6+ Kd8 14. Be3 ) 12. Rc1 h6 13. Bd2 { The same ideas continue. **Verdict:** the sources are club-level
(their win rates are quoted for players below 1800), so the evaluation is practical, not theoretical. Black's quiet
9...Qd8 keeps the extra pawns; White has the initiative, and the tactics above are what the opponent has to
avoid. } *

[Line "Milner-Barry: 9.Nbd2"]
[Section "5...Qb6 6.Bd3: Milner-Barry Gambit"]
[Summary "A quieter way to play the gambit: the knight keeps the f3-square and White does not immediately open the position."]

1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. e5 c5 4. c3 Nc6 5. Nf3 Qb6 6. Bd3 cxd4 7. cxd4 Bd7 8. O-O Nxd4 9. Nbd2 { [%cal Gd2b3,Gf3d4] White keeps
a knight on f3 and asks Black what he will do with the one on d4. Marin lists this as one of three ninth moves. }
9... Ne7 { Black develops and allows the trade. } ( 9... Nxf3+ 10. Nxf3 Bc5 { [%cal Gb2b4] } 11. b4!? { The idea is to open
the b-file. } 11... Bxb4?! { Takes a pawn but lets the rook onto the open b-file. } 12. Rb1 Qa5 13. a3 { The rook gets to b7. White is a pawn down but has compensation (Chess in
the Library). } 13... Bxa3?? { Loses a piece. } 14. Ra1 )
( 9... Nc6 { Black retreats. } 10. Nb3 { [%cal Gb3c5,Gc1e3] } 10... Nge7 11. Be3 Qc7 12. Rc1 Ng6 13. Nc5 { **Idea:** the knight
lands on c5 with tempo and eyes b7. Chess in the Library gives 13...Be7 14.b4 as one continuation, with space and pressure on the queenside. } 13... Ngxe5
14. Nxe5 Qxe5 15. Nxb7 { Chess in the Library: a sound sacrifice that is hard to defend in practice, with a 65 percent White
score in an amateur database. } )
10. Nxd4 Qxd4 11. Nf3 { The queen is hit and White develops with tempo. The source does not evaluate the position. } *

[Line "Milner-Barry: 9.Ng5 (Sorensen)"]
[Section "5...Qb6 6.Bd3: Milner-Barry Gambit"]
[Summary "Instead of taking on d4, the knight jumps to g5. It is slightly dubious but scores very well in practice, and the mating tactics are worth knowing."]

1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. e5 c5 4. c3 Nc6 5. Nf3 Qb6 6. Bd3 cxd4 7. cxd4 Bd7 8. O-O Nxd4 9. Ng5 { [%cal Gg5f7,Gd1h5] The Sorensen
variation. The Lichess study 'French as White' calls it slightly dubious, but says it has the highest win rate among
1200-1800 players. Marin lists 9.Ng5 as one of three ninth moves. } 9... h6 { About half of the time. } 10. Nxf7 { [%cal Gd1h5]
The knight sacrifice that the study demonstrates (it also gives 10.Qh5). } 10... Kxf7 11. Qh5+ Ke7 12. Qh4+ { If 12...Ke8 there is a bishop mate
in one; the king must go to f7 (rarely found), and White can repeat or try Be3. } 12... g5 13. Bxg5+ hxg5 14. Qxg5+ Kf7
15. Bg6+ Kg7 16. Bh5+ Kh7 17. Qg6# { **Verdict:** a forced mate after the natural 9...h6 and 12...g5, but Black can
sidestep with 12...Kf7 and 12...Nf6, so it is a practical weapon, not a refutation. } *

[Line "6.Bd3 Bd7: 7.dxc5 trap and 7.O-O"]
[Section "5...Qb6 6.Bd3: Milner-Barry Gambit"]
[Summary "Black pre-empts the bishop check with 6...Bd7. White either castles and reaches the main line, or takes on c5 and sets a trap on b4."]

1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. e5 c5 4. c3 Nc6 5. Nf3 Qb6 6. Bd3 Bd7 { Black prevents Bb5+ before taking on d4. This is nowadays the
most played reply (Wikibooks). } 7. dxc5! { [%cal Gd4c5,Gf8c5] The quick trap of the 'French as White' study, which scores
57 percent for White there. White takes the pawn: the e5-pawn still has Nf3 behind it. }
( 7. O-O { Fine if you prefer: Black takes on d4 in 84 percent of the games and the main line follows. } 7... cxd4
8. cxd4 Nxd4 9. Nxd4 Qxd4 10. Nc3 Qxe5 11. Re1 )
7... Bxc5 8. O-O Nge7? { [%cal Gb2b4] A natural developing move, and a mistake. } 9. b4! { [%cal Gb4c5] The bishop is attacked and has no
safe square (Wikibooks says it is trapped). } 9... Bxb4 10. cxb4 Nxb4 { **Verdict:** the study stops with the bishop traded for
two pawns and the knight attacking the bishop on d3. If Black avoids 8...Nge7 there is nothing dubious about White's
position, and the study reports about a 50 percent score for White. } *
